          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exploring Mixtures and their Separation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.953,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 85"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.086,
            0.31,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.4.3 Suspensions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.108,
            0.776,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us look closely at the container shown in Fig. 5.2. No matter how well you stir, the sand particles can be seen clearly, unlike in a solution, where the solute particles are not visible. Such heterogeneous mixtures in which the solid particles (sand) do not dissolve but remain suspended throughout the bulk of the medium (water) are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "suspensions",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In the case of a suspension, the particles of the undissolved substance are larger in size than the particles present in the solution. Particles of a suspension are visible to the naked eye. Some other examples of suspension are sawdust suspended in water and tea leaves in water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.27,
            0.518,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How can we separate mud from water?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.291,
            0.775,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you leave a container of muddy water undisturbed, the heavier mud particles settle at the bottom and the water may still appear cloudy. You can filter the muddy water through a cotton cloth or filter paper. While some larger particles will be removed, the water often remains cloudy. This shows that filtration is not always enough to separate tiny particles. If the muddy water is still not clear even after keeping for some time, how can it be cleaned? In such cases, we use techniques, such as centrifugation and/or coagulation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.413,
            0.42,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us explore these processes!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.439,
            0.295,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A. Centrifugation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.461,
            0.664,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us understand this by playing a game. Choose a partner. Then, cross your arms and hold each other’s hands as shown in Fig. 5.18. Now spin around together. Do you feel as if you are being pulled outwards? A similar force helps to separate a mixture in a tube by "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "centrifugation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This process involves spinning a mixture in a tube at a high speed (Fig. 5.19). During centrifugation, the tubes become horizontal and the centrifugal force (outward force acting on a body moving in circular motion) causes the heavier particles to move outwards, where they settle at the bottom of the tube, while the lighter liquid remains at the top."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.681,
            0.435,
            0.758,
            0.476
          ],
